<section xml:id="section-hopitals-asymptotese">
  <title>SLO 3: L'Hôpital's Rule and Asymptotic Analysis</title>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="section-asymptotic-analysis"/>, I get an asymptotic
    ranking of common functions. Here is that ranking for your
    convenience. 
    <md>
      c \lt  \ln x \lt  \ldots \lt  x^{\frac{1}{3}} \lt
      x^{\frac{1}{2}} \lt  x \lt  x^2 \lt  x^3 \lt  \ldots \lt  e^x
      \lt   \ldots
    </md>
    This ranking is very useful, but where did it come from? How do I
    know that it is true? Like all parts of mathematics, to entirely
    trust the result I eventually need a proof. 
  </p>
  <p>
    We've just covered L'Hôpital's rule as a way to calculate limit.
    It turns out that L'Hôpital's rule is very convenient for proving
    results about the asymptotic ranking of functions. In this
    section, I'm going to show you some of those proofs. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The idea is this. If <m>f(x)</m> grows asymptoticallyslower than
    <m>g(x)</m>, by defintion this means that the following limit must
    be zero.
    <md>
      \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = 0 
    </md>
    If the two functions are both growing functions, then this limit
    is always an indeterminate form of type
    <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>, so L'Hopital's rule applies. It can
    be used to justify these limits. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <statement>
      I claim that the cubie <m>x^3 + 8x + 1</m> grows slover than the
      quartic <m>x^4 + 3x^2 + x - 9</m>. To justify this claim, I need
      to consider this limit. 
      <md>
        \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{x^3 + 8x + 1}{x^4 + 3x^2 + x
        - 9} 
      </md>
      I'm not going to use asymptotic analysis here: I'm trying to
      prove asymptotic analysis, so using it would be circular
      reasoning. Instead, this is an indeterminate form of type
      <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>, so I can us L'Hôpital's rule. I
      differentiate numerator and denominator. 
      <md>
        \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{3x^2 + 8}{4x^3 + 2x + 1}
      </md>
      This is an indeterminate form of the same type, so I do the same
      again. 
      <md>
        \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{6x}{12x^2 + 21}
      </md>
      And once more.
      <md>
        \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{6}{24x} = 0
      </md>
      Now this is a limit that can be directly evaluated. The
      numerator is constant and the denominator gets arbitrarily
      large, so this limit is zero. I have succesfully argued that the
      cubic is, indeed, slower than the quartic. 
    </statement>
  </example>
  <p>
    Working with examples is fine, but I'd like to have a more general
    justification of the entire order. I'm not going to justify all
    of it in this section, but I'll handle most of it with three
    proofs. All of these proof have the same style of setting up a
    limit and using L'Hôpital's rule, often iteratively, to complete
    the proof. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>p(x)</m> and <m>q(x)</m> are polynomials and the degree
        ot <m>p(x)</m> is lower than the degree of <m>q(x)</m>, then
        <m>p(x)</m> has a lower asymptotic order than <m>q(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Here is the limit that determines the relative asymptotic
        order.
        <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{p(x)}{q(x)}
        </md>
        Assume that the degree of <m>p(x)</m> is some number <m>k</m>.
        Then I can apply L'Hôpital's rule <m>k</m> time, since each
        time the expression will still be an indeterminate form. 
        <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\frac{d^k}{dx^k}
          p(x)}{\frac{d^k}{dx^k} q(x)}
        </md>
        But now, after <m>k</m> derivative, the degree of
        <m>\frac{d^k}{dx^k} p(x)</m> will be zero. Each derivaitve
        drops the degree by once, so starting with degree <m>k</m> and
        taking <m>k</m> derivatives results in a constant. The
        denominator, howver, has a degree higher than <m>k</m>, so it
        will still have a positive degree after <m>k</m> derivatives
        and will still be a growing function. The result is a limit
        with a constant numerator and growing denominator, so the
        limit is zero and the relative asymptotic ranking is
        established. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The exponential function <m>f(x) = e^x</m> grows faster than
        any polynomial. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Again, I consider the ratio in the limit. I'll let <m>p(x)</m>
        be some polynomial of degree <m>k</m>.
        <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{e^x}{p(x)}
        </md>
        This is an indeterminate form of type
        <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>, so I can apply L'Hôpital's rule.
        I'll apply the rule <m>k</m> time, since it will remain an
        indeterminate form all through that process. 
        <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\frac{d^k}{dx^k} e^x}{
          \frac{d^k}{dx^k} p(x)}
        </md>
        The numerator doesn't change at all: the exponential function
        is unaltered by the derivative. After <m>k</m> derivatives,
        the denominator (which had degree <m>k</m> to start with)
        becomes a constant. Therefore, I have a growing function
        divided by a constant. That lets met conclude the limit must
        be infinity. Since the limit is infinity, the numerator must
        have a higher asymptotic order than the denominator. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>r \gt 0</m> be any real constant. The logarithm
        function <m>f(x) = \ln x</m> grow slower than <m>x^r</m> for
        any constnat <m>r</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Again, I setup the appropriate limit. 
        <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\ln x}{x^r}
        </md>
        Both of these are growing functions, to this is an
        indeterminate form of type <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>. I
        apply L'Hôpital's rule. 
        <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\frac{d}{dx} \ln
          x}{\frac{d}{dx} x^r}
        </md>
        I take the derivatives and simplify the resulting nested
        fraction. 
        <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{\frac{1}{x}}{(rx^{r-1}} =
          \lim_{x \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{rx}
        </md>
        This is a decaying function and the limit is zero. I conclude
        that the logarithm does, in fact, grow slower than any
        positive power. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
</section>


